content.text:
Now check where update's rpm variable is used: the update code references `rpm` later in the section I replaced. Yes.

Also the constructor maybe needs no changes.

Check `stopRace` stops `this.lfo`, plus sources; dcBlock no need.

Potential issue: In `startRace`, osc nodes start at freq values 300, 301.4, 150, 450, 600, 75, then update sets them. Fine.

Now `_crackle` uses `this.raceBus` — fine.

Let me syntax check and then test audio via an OfflineAudioContext? We can use Playwright with WebAudio; actual sound can't be heard, but we can verify no runtime errors and node graph exists. Better: render a WAV using OfflineAudioContext in the page? The audio engine uses window.AudioContext in ensure(). Could monkeypatch window.AudioContext with OfflineAudioContext to capture output and compute spectrum. That's a robust verification: run the game's audio engine against an OfflineAudioContext, call startRace, simulate update() calls over time, startRendering, then analyze RMS and spectral centroid. We can then reason it sounds petrol-like (rich harmonics). Also we can save a WAV to listen? We can't listen, but analysis helps.

But note OfflineAudioContext has no currentTime advancing until rendering — update() called synchronously sees currentTime 0 always, so automation with setTargetAtTime at t=0 all stacked. We'd need to schedule updates via suspend/resume at intervals. OfflineAudioContext supports suspend(time) and resume. We can build a script that runs in browser: create OfflineAudioContext, patch window.AudioContext = class extends OfflineAudioContext? Simpler: set `audio.ctx = new OfflineAudioContext(2, 44100*6, 44100)` and `audio.master`... but ensure() creates master when ctx exists? ensure returns existing ctx only if this.ctx is set; if we set audio.ctx manually, `ensure()` returns it and startRace uses it. But this.master would be null → `this.raceBus.connect(this.master)` fails. So initialize by calling ensure() on a real AudioContext then swap? Alternatively create offline context and construct master/comp ourselves, assigning to audio.master.

Plan in-page:
```
const SR=44100, DUR=7;
const off = new OfflineAudioContext(2, SR*DUR, SR);
const a = window.__apexAudio; // need export
a.ctx = off;
a.master = off.createGain(); a.master.gain.value=0.55;
const comp = off.createDynamicsCompressor(); ...
a.master.connect(comp); comp.connect(off.destination);
a.muted=false;
a.running=false;
a.startRace();
// schedule updates at 60Hz via offline suspend
for (let i=1;i<DUR*30;i++){ off.suspend(i/30).then(()=>{ a.update({speed:..., throttle:..., ...}); off.resume(); }); }
const buffer = await off.startRendering();
```
Wait, startRendering must be called after scheduling suspends; suspension callbacks run during rendering until complete. Then compute RMS and spectral centroid via FFT? We can just compute zero-crossing rate and RMS, and maybe run through an AnalyserNode? In offline, analyser works but reading after render is not meaningful. We can compute simple DFT at key frequencies via Goertzel on the PCM to see harmonic content. Or just compute RMS and zero crossings to ensure sound is produced.

